  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Periodic showers and thunderstorms are expected \nthrough Thursday. Some storms may be severe through tonight, with \nthe best timing late this afternoon into the overnight hours.\n\nA potent shortwave trough across northern Ontario, Canada this \nmorning will fill in as it moves further east, entering the Quebec \nprovince. Meanwhile at the surface, a lingering synoptic from  will \ncontinue to remain rather stationary across the region today, lying \nbetween the southern shores of Lake Ontario and the northern shore \nof Lake Erie. As the low drifts east today, expect the surface front \nto gradually shift northward across Lake Ontario. Given the \nplacement of the front, areas south of the front are currently lying \nwithin the warm sector of the surface low and are experiencing a \nwarm, soupy airmass as seen by observations at 06Z, where \ntemperatures range in the mid 60s to near 70, with a 2 degree spread \nin temperatures and dewpoints.\n\nA cold front is progged to cross the area late today/tonight, \nhowever ahead of the frontal passage, the aforementioned high \nhumidity currently in place will play a contributing factor to the \npotential for heavy rainfall today and tonight.\n\nIsolated scattered showers/thunderstorms currently across the Hudson \nBay this morning, will continue to drop east-southeastward across \nthe Lake Ontario and into the Genesee Valley this morning. While the \nprevious evening's sounding depicted quite the elevated mixed layer, \nand models are depicting a downward trend, a few stronger storms may \nbecome severe, capable of producing damaging straight line wind \ngusts, will be possible around daybreak and into the morning hours.\n\nA couple more additional rounds of organized showers and \nthunderstorm are expected for the remainder of the day, with \nattention drawn to the late evening into the overnight hours. Deep \nlayer bulk shear will remain moderate to strong with some weak to \nmoderate CAPE (including elevated CAPE) due to moist isentropic lift \nahead of the aforementioned shortwave trough axis passage, along \nwith moist, low-level convergence ascent along both surface trough \naxes of the warm and cold fronts will support favorable \nenvironments for low-level vertical wind shear. The greatest \ncoverage for showers and thunderstorms is expected this afternoon \nand will last towards midnight, as daytime heating in the warm and \nmoist sector will yield moderate to strong boundary layer CAPE. This \nis also when  \n\n The greatest coverage of showers and thunderstorms is expected \nWednesday afternoon through early evening, when Several rounds of \nshowers and thunderstorms with periods of heavy rainfall are \nexpected Wednesday through Wednesday night. This is when severe \nstorms in the form of multicells, including bow echoes, and \nsupercells, appear most-likely. Damaging straight-line wind gusts \nand damaging hail up to golf ball-size are expected to be the main \nsevere convective weather hazards due to the expectation of steep \nlow-level lapse rates/large DCAPE and mid-level lapse rates near \n7C/km contributing to sizable MUCAPE in the hail growth zone. A \ntornado or two is possible since low-level winds may veer and \nstrengthen significantly with height, resulting in sizable surface-\nbased ESRH amidst favorably-low mixed layer LCL's. Localized flash \nflooding is also possible where showers and storms manage to train, \nwhich should be the case along the warm front and any outflow \nboundaries oriented roughly west-to-east, which will be largely \nparallel to westerly to west-northwesterly mean mid-level flow.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Quieter weather, overall, expected Thursday\nnight into Friday.\n\nMainly fair weather is expected Thursday night through Friday as \na surface high pressure ridge builds from northern ON and vicinity, \nbehind the cold front. However, northwesterly flow aloft and \nembedded shortwave troughs will continue to impact our region. Moist \nisentropic ascent ahead of the shortwave trough axes and low- level \nconvergence/moist ascent along surface trough axes attendant to the \nshortwaves may release weak, yet sufficient instability, including \nelevated CAPE, to generate isolated showers and thunderstorms, \nespecially Thursday night. Temperatures and humidity levels will \ndrop back down to tolerable levels with lows reaching mainly the \nmid 50's to lower 60's around daybreak Friday. Highs should reach \nthe 70's late Friday afternoon.",
